\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{RL for multi-turn LLM agents.}
GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}, originally designed for mathematical reasoning, has been adapted to multi-turn agents through step-independent windowing~\cite{ragen2025}, with subsequent work improving algorithms (GiGPO~\cite{feng2025gigpo}), reward design (Self-Guide~\cite{selfguide2026}), or training pipelines (SPEAR~\cite{spear2025}, DigiRL~\cite{digiRL2024}). These systems share a common assumption: that trajectory decomposition into fixed windows is a benign implementation choice. Our work shows this assumption is false---the windowing itself introduces a structural credit assignment pathology that no algorithmic improvement within the step-independent paradigm can fix.

\paragraph{Credit assignment in reinforcement learning.}
The tension between temporal granularity and credit accuracy has a long history. TD($\lambda$)~\cite{sutton1988td} and GAE~\cite{schulman2016gae} interpolate between one-step bootstrapping and Monte Carlo returns, using a learned value function to reduce variance while maintaining temporal specificity. REINFORCE~\cite{williams1992reinforce} uses full episode returns, trading granularity for unbiased gradients. In the LLM agent setting, these classical approaches face a new challenge: the ``steps'' are multi-token generation episodes, and the value function must estimate returns from high-dimensional text observations. Our analysis suggests that in the low-success-rate regime ($p < 0.2$) typical of agent tasks, the classical bias-variance tradeoff may not apply as expected: Proposition~2 shows that sign errors under windowing grow as $1-p$, potentially dominating the variance reduction that windowing provides. Our experiments confirm this empirically: coarse-but-correct episode-level gradients outperform fine-but-wrong step-level gradients across all tested conditions.

\paragraph{Credit assignment and sign misattribution evidence.}
Several independent studies corroborate our sign misattribution diagnosis from different angles. IRC~\cite{irc2026} reports per-turn advantages frequently disagree in sign with outcome advantages (14pp degradation). MC-GRPO~\cite{mcgrpo2025} measures a 5\% sign-flip rate accounting for ${\sim}$4\% accuracy loss. GRPO-$\lambda$~\cite{grpolambda2025} proves value estimation error grows with position. OAR~\cite{oar2026} identifies ``reward aliasing'' from coarse credit. These findings align with our Proposition~2: at low success rates, group-relative normalization collapses the advantage sign to an episode-outcome proxy regardless of local action quality. Our contribution is to formalize this as a structural property of the training paradigm (not an algorithmic bug) and to show it interacts with information loss in a vicious cycle.

\paragraph{Multi-turn credit assignment methods.}
Turn-PPO~\cite{turnppo2026} introduces per-turn value estimation, GAGPO~\cite{gagpo2026} uses generalized advantage with a turn-level critic, MT-GRPO~\cite{mtgrpo2025} applies Monte Carlo returns at the turn level, and SWEET-RL~\cite{sweetrl2025} combines step-wise rewards with episode-level training. These methods address the credit assignment problem by improving per-step signal quality. Our work is complementary: we provide the mechanistic diagnosis that explains \textit{why} step-independent training fails, which informs when finer-grained credit is safe to use (only after establishing correct gradient direction via episode-level baselines).

\paragraph{GRPO collapse phenomena.}
RAGEN-2~\cite{ragen2_2026} proves advantage collapse when within-prompt reward variance vanishes. GRPO-Collapse~\cite{grpocollapse2025} identifies a death spiral in Search-R1 where all tested models collapse within 300 steps. Gradient Coupling~\cite{gradientcoupling2025} argues the true failure is high input similarity across consecutive steps. These observations converge on structural instability in step-independent GRPO. Our dual-mechanism diagnosis unifies these reports: reward variance collapse (RAGEN-2) is a special case of our reward saturation analysis (Section~\ref{sec:theory}), and the death spiral (GRPO-Collapse) follows from the M1--M2 vicious cycle described in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.

