\paragraph{Mechanism interaction.}
The two mechanisms interact in a vicious cycle under step-independent training. Information loss (M1) lowers the effective success rate $p$ by preventing the policy from executing multi-step strategies that require cross-window context. A lower $p$ increases the fraction of wrong-sign updates under sign misattribution (M2), since $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \to p$ as $K$ grows (Proposition~2). As M2 degrades the policy, $p$ decreases further, amplifying the ceiling imposed by M1. This positive feedback loop explains why step-independent training does not merely slow convergence but produces active collapse---and why remedies targeting only one mechanism (e.g., increasing $\gamma$ to address magnitude decay) fail to prevent the spiral.

\paragraph{Direction correctness vs.\ credit granularity.}
Episode-level GRPO assigns uniform advantage to all tokens. At low success rates ($p{=}12.5\%$), direction correctness dominates: coarse-but-correct gradients produce slow convergence, while fine-but-wrong gradients produce divergence. As $p$ increases, the marginal value of per-step credit grows. Methods combining episode-level direction with finer credit---such as $\lambda$-annealing from $\lambda{=}1$ to $\lambda{<}1$ as the policy improves---represent a natural extension. Our Cell~B results (Section~\ref{sec:results}) demonstrate this tradeoff concretely: episode-level advantage with truncated context achieves 55\% training success (correct direction enables learning) but hits a 5.97\% OOD ceiling (coarse context limits generalization).

\paragraph{Why step-independent became the default.}
If step-independent training is fundamentally flawed for multi-turn agents, why has it become the community standard? We identify six contributing factors (detailed in Appendix~\ref{app:why_defaults}): (1)~context length constraints of smaller language models force windowing as a pragmatic response; (2)~token-level training inertia from language modeling carries over to RL without questioning the unit of optimization; (3)~framework design accident---RAGEN's default configuration uses step-independent mode, and defaults propagate silently; (4)~memory and compute savings ($O(h)$ vs.\ $O(T)$ per sequence) provide immediate benefits while credit assignment failure is subtle and deferred; (5)~dense reward shaping appears to address sparse reward but creates new gaming targets (Section~\ref{sec:results_dense}); and (6)~absence of published negative results prevents the community from distinguishing ``this problem is hard'' from ``this training paradigm is broken.''

\paragraph{Practitioner guidance.}
For multi-turn agent RL with sparse terminal reward, we recommend a staged approach: (1)~if the full episode fits within context, use episode-level credit as the minimum viable approach---this eliminates both M1 and M2 with no hyperparameter tuning; (2)~if the episode exceeds context capacity, use the largest feasible $h$ with episode-level advantage (our Cell~B configuration), accepting the information-loss ceiling while avoiding sign misattribution; (3)~finer-grained credit (e.g., Turn-PPO, GAGPO) should be considered only after establishing a working episode-level baseline, as these methods assume correct gradient direction as a prerequisite. Our window-size sweep (Section~\ref{sec:results_hsweep}) provides concrete guidance on the minimum $h$ required: for ALFWorld, $h{\geq}20$ is necessary for partial recovery, and $h{\leq}10$ leads to collapse regardless of other hyperparameters.

\paragraph{Limitations of the episode-level fix.}
Episode-level training is not a complete solution. It assigns uniform credit to all tokens, which becomes increasingly wasteful as episodes grow longer and the policy improves. At $p{=}90\%$ (our 8B endpoint), most trajectories succeed and the gradient signal primarily distinguishes ``good enough'' from ``slightly better,'' where per-step credit would provide more useful signal. Additionally, episode-level training requires $O(T)$ memory per sequence, which may be prohibitive for environments with very long trajectories ($T > 1000$). These limitations motivate hybrid approaches that begin with episode-level training (ensuring correct direction) and progressively introduce finer-grained credit as the policy stabilizes.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Multi-turn agent RL requires credit assignment across long interaction trajectories, yet current frameworks default to step-independent windowing that decomposes trajectories into independently optimized windows. We diagnose a dual mechanism underlying this failure: policy information loss at the inference layer, which imposes a constant performance ceiling, and advantage sign misattribution at the training layer, which causes cumulative degradation. Formal analysis shows that at a baseline success rate of $p = 12.5\%$ with group size $K = 8$, approximately 80.9\% of gradient updates for locally correct windows point in the wrong direction.

A $2{\times}2$ factorial experiment separates the two mechanisms. Eliminating sign misattribution (Cell~B) converts catastrophic collapse into bounded learning, confirming the optimizer can learn from truncated windows when gradient signs are correct. Removing the information-loss ceiling (Cell~D) enables generalization, reaching 17.2\% mean OOD success at 1.7B (two seeds; range 10.4--23.9\%) and 90.3\% at 8B. A window-size sweep reveals a credit assignment cliff between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ that separates conditions that learn from those that collapse. Cross-environment validation on ScienceWorld confirms the collapse generalizes beyond ALFWorld.

The central insight is that sign errors are more destructive than magnitude imprecision. This distinction---that gradients pointing in the wrong direction cause active divergence, while gradients with imprecise magnitude merely slow convergence---applies to any agent RL method combining step-level windowing, sparse binary reward, and group-relative advantage normalization. A natural next step is $\lambda$-annealing: beginning with episode-level training ($\lambda{=}1$) to establish correct gradient direction, then progressively introducing finer-grained credit ($\lambda{<}1$) as the policy stabilizes and the marginal value of per-step signal grows.

