\section{Theoretical Analysis}
\label{sec:theory}

We formalize why sign misattribution is the more fundamental failure mechanism and derive the probability of incorrect gradient direction. Full proofs appear in Appendix~\ref{app:theory_full}.

\textbf{Proposition 1.} \textit{Under step-independent training with binary episode reward $R_{\text{episode}} \in \{0, r_+\}$, the probability that window $k$'s advantage $\hat{A}_k$ has the correct sign (aligned with local action quality) approaches the base success rate $p$ as the number of rollouts $K$ per group increases.}

\textit{Proof sketch.} In a failed episode ($R_{\text{episode}} = 0$), all windows receive $R_k = 0$. If any rollout in the group succeeded, $\mu > 0$, so every window in the failed episode receives $\hat{A}_k < 0$---including windows with locally correct actions. The probability of receiving the wrong sign in a failed episode is $1 - (1-p)^{K-1}$ (at least one group member succeeded). As $K$ grows, this approaches $1-p$, and the overall probability of correct sign approaches $p$. \hfill$\square$

The key distinction is temporal: information loss (M1) imposes a constant ceiling determined by $h$, while sign misattribution (M2) is cumulative---each wrong-sign gradient step further erodes policy competence below that ceiling. A gradient with the wrong sign causes the optimizer to move \textit{away} from good policies, while a gradient with reduced magnitude due to discounting ($\gamma^{(N-k)}$) still points toward good policies, just with a smaller step. Sign errors produce active divergence; magnitude errors merely slow convergence (Appendix~\ref{app:sign_fundamental}).

\textbf{Proposition 2.}\label{prop:correct_sign} \textit{Let $p$ denote the episode success rate and $K$ the number of rollouts per group. Under step-independent training with binary episode reward, the fraction of gradient updates with correct sign is:}
\begin{equation}
P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) = \frac{p}{1 - (1-p)^{K}}
\label{eq:correct_conditional}
\end{equation}

\textit{Derivation.} Three mutually exclusive cases determine gradient direction for a window with locally good actions: (1)~episode succeeded (probability $p$): correct sign; (2)~episode failed but group contains a success (probability $(1{-}p)[1{-}(1{-}p)^{K-1}]$): wrong sign; (3)~entire group failed (probability $(1{-}p)^K$): zero gradient. Conditioning on non-zero gradient (Cases 1 and 2) yields Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional}.

At our experimental baseline ($p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$), only 19.1\% of gradient updates have the correct sign. In the large-$K$ limit, $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \to p$, so ${\sim}87.5\%$ of updates for locally correct windows point in the wrong direction.

\paragraph{Severity scales with task difficulty.} Equation~\ref{eq:correct_conditional} reveals a counterintuitive property: sign misattribution becomes \textit{more} severe as the task gets harder. At $p{=}50\%$, $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) = 50.2\%$---barely above random. At $p{=}5\%$ (a difficult task), only 5.1\% of updates are correct. Multi-turn agent tasks with low baseline success rates are precisely the settings where credit assignment is most needed, yet where step-independent training produces the worst gradient signal. Increasing $K$ does not help: it ensures more updates occur (fewer all-fail groups), but each update is equally likely to point in the wrong direction.

\paragraph{Reward saturation.} A complementary failure mode arises when all $K$ rollouts in a group receive the same binary reward. In this case, $\sigma = 0$ and $\hat{A}_k = 0$ for all windows, producing zero gradient (no learning). At $p{=}12.5\%$, the probability that all $K{=}8$ rollouts fail is $(1{-}p)^K \approx 34.4\%$. Combined with the rare all-success case ($p^K < 10^{-7}$), approximately one-third of training batches produce no gradient at all. This compounds the sign misattribution problem: of the ${\sim}66\%$ of batches that produce a gradient, only 19.1\% have the correct sign. The effective learning rate is thus $0.66 \times 0.19 \approx 12.6\%$ of what the optimizer assumes---substantially below the regime where convergence guarantees hold.
